{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"STEP 0 DESIGN DECISION, MEASURED — the plan requires picking one threading mechanism and recording why. Plan PASSED round 5 (0 blocking, 3 advisory) after R2/R3/R4; re-signed and certified at 44d9250ea.\n\n**CHOSEN: (c) a ContextVar set by the step executor, read at `runner.py:547`.** Both the step id AND the raw declared class token ride on it.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT THAT DECIDED IT: **`RunRequest` is constructed at 25 sites under `src/rebar`** — spec_scan.py:123, enrich.py:81, operations.py:177, eval_solver.py:164 and :233, xcheck.py:199 and :337, prerequisites.py:228, plan_review/__init__.py:164, and more. Most have NO step context at all (a spec scan and an enrich pass are not workflow steps).\n* (a) a new `RunRequest` field — rejected on that count. Required would churn all 25; optional would leave ~24 sites passing `None` forever, which is noise that makes the field look meaningless at every site that legitimately has no step.\n* (b) overloading the existing `target: dict` — rejected: `target` means the thing under review, not the caller. Reusing it would save a field and cost a reader's understanding of both.\n* (c) ContextVar — zero churn at all 25 sites, and it scopes naturally: the executor sets it around one step's execution and drops it after, so a call made OUTSIDE a step simply has no step id, which is the truthful answer rather than a placeholder. **Direct in-repo precedent:** `_active_gate_config` (`llm/config.py:88`), whose own comment says it mirrors \"the active read-root ContextVars in `rebar.llm.gate_context`\". So this is the established way this codebase threads run-scoped context to a deep chokepoint.\n\nWHAT `record()` GAINS (additive, following its existing `if model:` pattern so absent values are simply omitted rather than written as null): `step` and `model_class`. `model_class` is written ONLY when the raw declared token is one of `CLASS_NAMES` — a literal model id is not a class, and recording it as one would recreate the ambiguity Step 0 exists to remove.\n\nNEXT: held-out TDD — write the attribution test FIRST, confirm RED for the right reason, then implement. The test must assert on a log produced by an actual gate run (per the AC), not on `usage_log.py`'s source, because reading the writer cannot show that the step id ARRIVES."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785612822770044001,"uuid":"197c2362-a6b1-479b-ad19-8798e285e5dc"}